From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 17:24:57 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: restrict the coherency workaround to loops

Large compute kernels miscompile on macOS 26 when a global access is marked
coherent: part way through the kernel every device load starts returning zero,
and the point where that begins moves earlier as the thread index grows. The
zeroed loads include descriptor-set reads, so sampler and buffer descriptors
collapse to zero and the affected invocations sample the wrong texture or drop
their stores. The memory is intact when the host inspects it after the
submission, and volatile reproduces the same failure, so the trigger is the
re-issued load rather than the coherency qualifier.

Emulated tessellation evaluation, geometry and transform feedback stages re-run
the application shader as a compute kernel and are large enough to hit this.
KK_WORKAROUND_6 marked every global access coherent, which is what makes those
kernels re-issue their loads.

Restrict KK_WORKAROUND_6 to accesses inside a loop, which is the only place the
memory coherency issue it works around can be observed, and record the new
issue as KK_WORKAROUND_15 so the previous unconditional marking can still be
selected.

Add a KosmicKrisp test that draws isolines through a tessellation evaluation
shader indexing a sampler array with a uniform value, which line rasterization
materializes as a compute kernel.
